1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:10,510
Two weeks ago, the rule was simple: never boil your broccoli, because heat destroys the enzyme that makes its most important molecule.

2
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:16,110
Today, I am going to tell you to cook your tomatoes.

3
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:23,070
And the reason those two statements do not contradict each other is the whole point of this video.

4
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:27,150
A raw tomato is not the best version of a tomato.

5
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:28,430
Cooking it

6
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:39,550
The thing everyone tells you destroys nutrients, makes its most valuable molecule several times the house for your body to actually use.

7
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:43,950
The red in a tomato is a pigment called lycopene.

8
00:00:44,510 --> 00:00:49,230
It belongs to the same family as the beta carotene that makes carrots orange.

9
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:53,470
But inside a raw tomato, lycopene is not free.

10
00:00:54,230 --> 00:01:01,350
It sits inside structures called chromoplasts, bound into a matrix of protein and membrane.

11
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:05,190
Your digestion is not good at breaking that open.

12
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:14,390
Eat a raw tomato, and much of the lycopene passes through you still locked inside the fragments of plant cell it arrived in.

13
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:17,910
Heat does three things to that.

14
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:21,830
First, it ruptures the cell walls.

15
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:23,670
Mechanical release.

16
00:01:24,470 --> 00:01:29,510
The pigment is no longer trapped behind cellulose your enzymes cannot cut.

17
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:39,750
Second, it denatures the proteins holding the pigment in place, so the molecule comes free rather than staying bound to a structure.

18
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:46,070
Third, and this is the interesting one, heat changes the shape of the molecule itself.

19
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:55,030
In a raw tomato, most lycopene is a straight, rod-like form, called the trans isomer.

20
00:01:55,910 --> 00:02:08,150
Heat bends it into cis isomers, and that bent shape packs far more easily into the micelles that carry fat-soluble molecules across your intestinal wall.

21
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,510
Straight molecules stack badly.

22
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:13,510
Bent ones fit.

23
00:02:14,350 --> 00:02:20,990
so the same amount of lycopene, cooked, reaches your bloodstream in much larger quantities.

24
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:32,830
In controlled human studies, lycopene from tomato paste was absorbed several times better than lycopene from an equivalent portion of fresh tomatoes.

25
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:35,310
There is a second multiplier.

26
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:41,710
Lycopene is fat-soluble, so without fat, there are no micelles waiting for it.

27
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:45,830
Cooking tomatoes in olive oil is not just flavor.

28
00:02:46,390 --> 00:02:48,310
It completes the delivery system.

29
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:51,430
Same mechanism we covered with avocado.

30
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:52,950
The fat is not the nutrient.

31
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:54,670
The fat is the vehicle.

32
00:02:55,910 --> 00:02:57,390
Now follow it past the intestine.

33
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:07,030
Absorbed lycopene is packed into chylomicrons, enters the lymphatic system, and reaches your bloodstream.

34
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:11,350
From there, it transfers into your lipoproteins.

35
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:12,910
And this is where it gets specific.

36
00:03:14,470 --> 00:03:18,710
Lycopene concentrates inside LDL particles.

37
00:03:19,510 --> 00:03:20,310
That matters.

38
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:25,670
Because the first step in arterial plaque is not LDL itself.

39
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:31,830
It is LDL becoming oxidized inside the artery wall.

40
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:40,630
An oxidized LDL particle is what a macrophage swallows uncontrollably to become a foam cell.

41
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:46,670
Lycopene riding inside that particle sits exactly where the damage starts.

42
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:49,390
It also builds up in tissue.

43
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:57,790
The liver, the adrenal glands, the testes and the prostate hold the highest concentrations in the body.

44
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:00,750
So what does the evidence say?

45
00:04:01,790 --> 00:04:03,950
Here it splits, and I want to be careful.

46
00:04:05,310 --> 00:04:08,510
On observational data, it is consistent.

47
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:21,470
People with higher lycopene intake, and more importantly higher lycopene measured in their blood, show lower rates of cardiovascular disease across many populations.

48
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:24,590
Meta-analyses of those studies agree.

49
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:27,630
On randomized trials, it is thinner.

50
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:36,590
Supplementing isolated lycopene has not reliably reproduced the benefits seen in people who eat tomatoes.

51
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:39,910
That gap usually means one of two things.

52
00:04:40,390 --> 00:04:46,710
Either the whole food matters more than the single molecule, or the association was never fully causal.

53
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,510
We do not yet know which.

54
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:54,310
On prostate cancer, be skeptical of what you have heard.

55
00:04:54,870 --> 00:05:01,110
The claim was strong in the 1990s, and has been steadily downgraded as better studies arrived.

56
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:04,270
Regulators now describe that evidence as very limited.

57
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:06,790
Mechanism.

58
00:05:07,230 --> 00:05:07,870
Strong.

59
00:05:09,070 --> 00:05:11,390
Population data, consistent.

60
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:15,150
Isolated supplement trials, disappointing.

61
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:17,310
So, four rules.

62
00:05:17,710 --> 00:05:18,190
One.

63
00:05:18,910 --> 00:05:19,550
Cook them.

64
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:24,030
Sauce, roasted, stewed, or paste.

65
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:31,470
The processed form is genuinely the better sauce here, which is rare enough to be worth saying out loud.

66
00:05:32,830 --> 00:05:33,310
Two.

67
00:05:34,030 --> 00:05:35,230
Cook them in oil.

68
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:38,830
Without fat, the lycopene has no way across.

69
00:05:40,030 --> 00:05:40,510
3.

70
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:46,270
Tomato paste is the most concentrated lycopene source in an ordinary kitchen.

71
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:50,990
One spoonful carries more than several fresh tomatoes.

72
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:52,430
4.

73
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:57,710
Keep some raw, because cooking does cost you vitamin C.

74
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:00,590
This is a real trade, not a free upgrade.

75
00:06:02,070 --> 00:06:06,070
cooked for lycopene, raw for vitamin C, and eat both.

76
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:07,830
Two quick notes.

77
00:06:08,230 --> 00:06:13,110
Very high long-term intake can tint the skin faintly orange.

78
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:15,830
It is harmless, and it reverses.

79
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:24,070
And if you have reflux, tomato is a common trigger, which has nothing to do with lycopene and everything to do with acidity.

80
00:06:24,830 --> 00:06:26,910
None of this was really about a tomato.

81
00:06:27,310 --> 00:06:37,150
It was about a molecule that is present but unavailable, and the fact that how you prepare your food decides which of those two it is.

82
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:40,030
So which molecule should we follow next?

83
00:06:41,390 --> 00:06:47,150
Write a food and an organ in the comments, and we will take it apart, cell by cell.
